For every product of finite input alphabets, the rule $\mathcal W$ has finite range, and each output indicator is a finite linear combination of functions of at most $m$ input coordinates.*


*For independent centered variance-one inputs, whose marginal laws need not agree, and every fixed $b\in\mathbb R$, put $$\Delta_b=\mathbb E[\mathbf1_A(1-(L-b)^2)].$$ Then $$\label{eq:retained}
 \operatorname{Var}(\mathbb E[L\mid\mathcal W])\ge m+\Delta_b.$$

On every product of finite input alphabets, $\mathcal O$ has finite range, and each output indicator is a finite linear combination of functions of at most $m$ input coordinates.*


*Let the inputs be independent, centered, and of variance one; their marginal laws need not agree. For any fixed constants $b_0,b_1\in\mathbb R$, define $$\label{eq:coarse-deficit}
 g_{b_0,b_1}
 =\mathbf 1_A\bigl(1-(L-b_0)^2\bigr)
   +\mathbf 1_B\bigl(1-(L-b_1)^2\bigr).$$ Then the optimal residual error $\mathcal R=\mathbb E[(L-\mathbb E[L\mid\mathcal O])^2]$ satisfies $$\label{eq:coarse-residual}
 1-\mathcal R\ge\mathbb Eg_{b_0,b_1},\qquad
 \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}(\mathbb E[L\mid\mathcal O])\ge m+\mathbb Eg_{b_0,b_1}.$$
